5.Quick Sort

Quick Sort also uses divide and conquer, like Merge Sort, but instead of merging two sorted halves, 
it chooses a pivot and places smaller elements on its left and larger elements on its right.


def quick_sort(arr, low, high):
    if low < high:
        pivot = arr[high]
        i = low - 1

        for j in range(low, high):
            if arr[j] <= pivot:
                i += 1
                arr[i], arr[j] = arr[j], arr[i]

        arr[i + 1], arr[high] = arr[high], arr[i + 1]
        p = i + 1

        quick_sort(arr, low, p - 1)
        quick_sort(arr, p + 1, high)

    return arr


arr = [9, 13, 20, 24, 46, 52]
print(quick_sort(arr, 0, len(arr) - 1))


Case	Time Complexity
Best case	= O(n\log n)
Average case	= O(n\log n)
Worst case	=  O(n^2)

Space Complexity:
- Average:  O(\log n) — recursion stack.
- Worst:   O(n)— recursion stack when partitions are highly unbalanced.
Why is the worst case  O(n^2)? When the pivot repeatedly becomes the smallest or largest element, the partitions become very uneven.
Interview note: Our implementation sorts the array in place, so it doesn't create extra arrays. Its extra space is mainly used by recursion.
